"James loves to go swimming and has to swim across a 20-mile lake. He can swim at a pace of 2 miles per hour. He swims 60% of the distance. After that, He stops on an island and rests for half as long as the swimming time. He then finishes the remaining distance while going half the speed. How long did it take him to get across the lake?","James loves to go swimming and has to swim across a 20-mile lake. He can swim at a pace of 2 miles per hour. He swims 60% of the distance. After that, he stops on an island and rests for half as long as the swimming time. He then finishes the remaining distance while going half the speed. How long did it take him to get across the lake?",17,17,17,17,"Step 1: Determine initial swimming distance and time.
- Total distance: 20 miles
- Speed initially: 2 mph
- He swims 60% of the distance: 0.60 × 20 = 12 miles
- Time for first 12 miles: 12 miles ÷ 2 mph = 6 hours

Step 2: Rest time.
- Rest is half as long as the swimming time: 0.5 × 6 = 3 hours

Step 3: Remaining distance and reduced speed.
- Remaining distance: 20 − 12 = 8 miles
- New speed is half of 2 mph: 1 mph
- Time for remaining 8 miles: 8 miles ÷ 1 mph = 8 hours

Step 4: Total time.
- Total time = swimming time (first part) + rest + swimming time (second part)
- Total time = 6 + 3 + 8 = 17 hours

